{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-76416" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}2{x}^{2}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$ for $\\,1\\le x\\le 2e?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6d5e6f80d4df795a749d7ac87e1a0666579b900c6d042388e113c660bec8d65"} {"question" "q-76416" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t).$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le 2e\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t))j \\\\  & =i+\\frac{1}{t}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}{e}^{y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2{t}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}=2{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{2e}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2e}2{t}^{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{1}^{2e}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6d5e6f80d4df795a749d7ac87e1a0666579b900c6d042388e113c660bec8d65"} {"question" "q-76416" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a3cbc1b157fde6ab3c8cdd7b820e04bf0f9c2372ad52cb4dd5f48ef710985cbe"} {"question" "q-76416" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6d5e6f80d4df795a749d7ac87e1a0666579b900c6d042388e113c660bec8d65"} {"question" "q-76416" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{\\int }_{1}^{2e}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "2{\\int }_{1}^{2e}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "2{\\int }_{1}^{2e}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{t}}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "2{\\int }_{1}^{2e}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "2{\\int }_{1}^{2e}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6d5e6f80d4df795a749d7ac87e1a0666579b900c6d042388e113c660bec8d65"} {"question" "q-76416" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2{\\int }_{1}^{2e}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6d5e6f80d4df795a749d7ac87e1a0666579b900c6d042388e113c660bec8d65"} {"question" "q-148206" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}xy\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $y={x}^{3}$ between the points $(0,0)$ and $(2,8)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6d5e6f80d4df795a749d7ac87e1a0666579b900c6d042388e113c660bec8d65"} {"question" "q-148206" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)={t}^{3}.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{t}^{3}⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})j \\\\  & =i+3{t}^{2}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{(3{t}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+9{t}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=xy$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =t\\cdot {t}^{3}={t}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{4}\\sqrt{1+9{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6d5e6f80d4df795a749d7ac87e1a0666579b900c6d042388e113c660bec8d65"} {"question" "q-148206" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a3cbc1b157fde6ab3c8cdd7b820e04bf0f9c2372ad52cb4dd5f48ef710985cbe"} {"question" "q-148206" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6d5e6f80d4df795a749d7ac87e1a0666579b900c6d042388e113c660bec8d65"} {"question" "q-148206" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{1+9{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "4{\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2}t\\sqrt{9+{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{4}\\sqrt{1+9{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6d5e6f80d4df795a749d7ac87e1a0666579b900c6d042388e113c660bec8d65"} {"question" "q-148206" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{4}\\sqrt{1+9{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6d5e6f80d4df795a749d7ac87e1a0666579b900c6d042388e113c660bec8d65"} {"question" "q-148205" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $y=2{e}^{x}$ for $\\,0\\le x\\le 2?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6d5e6f80d4df795a749d7ac87e1a0666579b900c6d042388e113c660bec8d65"} {"question" "q-148205" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=2{e}^{t}.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,2{e}^{t}⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{t})j \\\\  & =i+2{e}^{t}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(2{e}^{t})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4{e}^{2t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\sqrt{1+4{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{1+4{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6d5e6f80d4df795a749d7ac87e1a0666579b900c6d042388e113c660bec8d65"} {"question" "q-148205" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a3cbc1b157fde6ab3c8cdd7b820e04bf0f9c2372ad52cb4dd5f48ef710985cbe"} {"question" "q-148205" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6d5e6f80d4df795a749d7ac87e1a0666579b900c6d042388e113c660bec8d65"} {"question" "q-148205" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{1+4{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{1+{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "4{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{1+4{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{1+4{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{1+4{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6d5e6f80d4df795a749d7ac87e1a0666579b900c6d042388e113c660bec8d65"} {"question" "q-148205" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{1+4{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6d5e6f80d4df795a749d7ac87e1a0666579b900c6d042388e113c660bec8d65"} {"question" "q-148208" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}2{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $y=\\frac{1}{x}$ between the points $(1,1)$ and $(2,\\frac{1}{2})?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6d5e6f80d4df795a749d7ac87e1a0666579b900c6d042388e113c660bec8d65"} {"question" "q-148208" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=\\frac{1}{t}.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\frac{1}{t}⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})j \\\\  & =i-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+1}}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2{x}^{3}y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2\\cdot {t}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}=2{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2{t}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+1}}{{t}^{2}}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6d5e6f80d4df795a749d7ac87e1a0666579b900c6d042388e113c660bec8d65"} {"question" "q-148208" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a3cbc1b157fde6ab3c8cdd7b820e04bf0f9c2372ad52cb4dd5f48ef710985cbe"} {"question" "q-148208" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6d5e6f80d4df795a749d7ac87e1a0666579b900c6d042388e113c660bec8d65"} {"question" "q-148208" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+1}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "2{\\int }_{1}^{2}t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "2{\\int }_{1}^{2}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+1}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "{\\int }_{1}^{2}t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+2}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{t}^{4}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6d5e6f80d4df795a749d7ac87e1a0666579b900c6d042388e113c660bec8d65"} {"question" "q-148208" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+1}\\,\\text{d}t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6d5e6f80d4df795a749d7ac87e1a0666579b900c6d042388e113c660bec8d65"} {"question" "q-76414" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}y{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $y=5x+2$ for $\\,0\\le x\\le 1?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6d5e6f80d4df795a749d7ac87e1a0666579b900c6d042388e113c660bec8d65"} {"question" "q-76414" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=5t+2.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,5t+2⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 1\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5t+2)j \\\\  & =i+5\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{26}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=y{e}^{x}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(5t+2){e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(5t+2){e}^{t}\\cdot \\sqrt{26}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{26}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(5t+2){e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6d5e6f80d4df795a749d7ac87e1a0666579b900c6d042388e113c660bec8d65"} {"question" "q-76414" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a3cbc1b157fde6ab3c8cdd7b820e04bf0f9c2372ad52cb4dd5f48ef710985cbe"} {"question" "q-76414" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6d5e6f80d4df795a749d7ac87e1a0666579b900c6d042388e113c660bec8d65"} {"question" "q-76414" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\sqrt{26}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(5t+2){e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "\\sqrt{26}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(t+2){e}^{5t}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "\\sqrt{26}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{5t+2}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(5t+2){e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(5t+2){e}^{t}\\sqrt{26t}\\,\\text{d}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6d5e6f80d4df795a749d7ac87e1a0666579b900c6d042388e113c660bec8d65"} {"question" "q-76414" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{26}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(5t+2){e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6d5e6f80d4df795a749d7ac87e1a0666579b900c6d042388e113c660bec8d65"} {"question" "q-148215" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=2xy$ along the curve $y=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}},$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6d5e6f80d4df795a749d7ac87e1a0666579b900c6d042388e113c660bec8d65"} {"question" "q-148215" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\,\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(u) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}})j \\\\  & =i-\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{(-\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4-{t}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2xy$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2t\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2t\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2}t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2({2}^{2}-{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6d5e6f80d4df795a749d7ac87e1a0666579b900c6d042388e113c660bec8d65"} {"question" "q-148215" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a3cbc1b157fde6ab3c8cdd7b820e04bf0f9c2372ad52cb4dd5f48ef710985cbe"} {"question" "q-148215" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6d5e6f80d4df795a749d7ac87e1a0666579b900c6d042388e113c660bec8d65"} {"question" "q-148215" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "4\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "8"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{8}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6d5e6f80d4df795a749d7ac87e1a0666579b900c6d042388e113c660bec8d65"} {"question" "q-148215" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6d5e6f80d4df795a749d7ac87e1a0666579b900c6d042388e113c660bec8d65"} {"question" "q-76483" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=2{y}^{2}$ along the curve $y={e}^{x},$ $\\,0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6d5e6f80d4df795a749d7ac87e1a0666579b900c6d042388e113c660bec8d65"} {"question" "q-76483" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)={e}^{t}.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\,{e}^{t}⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(u) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})j \\\\  & =i+{e}^{t}j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{({e}^{t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2({e}^{t})^{2}=2{e}^{2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}2{e}^{2t}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}2{e}^{2t}\\,\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=1+{e}^{2t}$, $\\text{d}u=2{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}2{e}^{2t}\\,\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{2}^{10}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}{|}_{2}^{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}({10}^{3/2}-{2}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(10\\sqrt{10}-2\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}(5\\sqrt{10}-\\sqrt{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6d5e6f80d4df795a749d7ac87e1a0666579b900c6d042388e113c660bec8d65"} {"question" "q-76483" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a3cbc1b157fde6ab3c8cdd7b820e04bf0f9c2372ad52cb4dd5f48ef710985cbe"} {"question" "q-76483" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6d5e6f80d4df795a749d7ac87e1a0666579b900c6d042388e113c660bec8d65"} {"question" "q-76483" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "2(5\\sqrt{10}-\\sqrt{2})"] ["math" "5\\sqrt{10}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{3}(5\\sqrt{10}-\\sqrt{2})"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{3}(5\\sqrt{10}-\\sqrt{2})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6d5e6f80d4df795a749d7ac87e1a0666579b900c6d042388e113c660bec8d65"} {"question" "q-76483" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{4}{3}(5\\sqrt{10}-\\sqrt{2})"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6d5e6f80d4df795a749d7ac87e1a0666579b900c6d042388e113c660bec8d65"} {"question" "q-76431" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is given by $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4},$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 4\\sqrt{6}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6d5e6f80d4df795a749d7ac87e1a0666579b900c6d042388e113c660bec8d65"} {"question" "q-76431" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\,\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 4\\sqrt{6}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(u) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4})j \\\\  & =i+\\frac{t}{2}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{(\\frac{t}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{y}{x}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4})}{t}=\\frac{t}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{4\\sqrt{6}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4\\sqrt{6}}\\frac{t}{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\sqrt{6}}t\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=1+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}$, $\\text{d}u=\\frac{t}{2}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\sqrt{6}}t\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}}\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{25}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{3/2}}{3}{|}_{1}^{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({25}^{3/2}-{1}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(125-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{124}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6d5e6f80d4df795a749d7ac87e1a0666579b900c6d042388e113c660bec8d65"} {"question" "q-76431" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a3cbc1b157fde6ab3c8cdd7b820e04bf0f9c2372ad52cb4dd5f48ef710985cbe"} {"question" "q-76431" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6d5e6f80d4df795a749d7ac87e1a0666579b900c6d042388e113c660bec8d65"} {"question" "q-76431" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{124}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{125}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{25}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6d5e6f80d4df795a749d7ac87e1a0666579b900c6d042388e113c660bec8d65"} {"question" "q-76431" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{124}{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6d5e6f80d4df795a749d7ac87e1a0666579b900c6d042388e113c660bec8d65"} {"question" "q-148219" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x-y}$ along the line $y=x-4,$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6d5e6f80d4df795a749d7ac87e1a0666579b900c6d042388e113c660bec8d65"} {"question" "q-148219" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=t-4.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\,t-4⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(u) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-4)j \\\\  & =i+\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x-y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{1}{t-(t-4))}=\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\,t\\,{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}(2-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6d5e6f80d4df795a749d7ac87e1a0666579b900c6d042388e113c660bec8d65"} {"question" "q-148219" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a3cbc1b157fde6ab3c8cdd7b820e04bf0f9c2372ad52cb4dd5f48ef710985cbe"} {"question" "q-148219" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6d5e6f80d4df795a749d7ac87e1a0666579b900c6d042388e113c660bec8d65"} {"question" "q-148219" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6d5e6f80d4df795a749d7ac87e1a0666579b900c6d042388e113c660bec8d65"} {"question" "q-148219" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6d5e6f80d4df795a749d7ac87e1a0666579b900c6d042388e113c660bec8d65"} {"question" "q-148218" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=({x}^{2}+y)$ along the line $y=3x+2,$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6d5e6f80d4df795a749d7ac87e1a0666579b900c6d042388e113c660bec8d65"} {"question" "q-148218" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=3t+2.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\,3t+2⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 1\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(u) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t+2)j \\\\  & =i+3\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={t}^{2}+(3t+2)={t}^{2}+3t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+3t+2)\\cdot \\sqrt{10}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+3t+2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}\\,(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{2}+2t){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}(\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{3}{2}+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{23\\sqrt{10}}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6d5e6f80d4df795a749d7ac87e1a0666579b900c6d042388e113c660bec8d65"} {"question" "q-148218" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a3cbc1b157fde6ab3c8cdd7b820e04bf0f9c2372ad52cb4dd5f48ef710985cbe"} {"question" "q-148218" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6d5e6f80d4df795a749d7ac87e1a0666579b900c6d042388e113c660bec8d65"} {"question" "q-148218" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "13\\sqrt{10}"] ["math" "3\\sqrt{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{23\\sqrt{10}}{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{6}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6d5e6f80d4df795a749d7ac87e1a0666579b900c6d042388e113c660bec8d65"} {"question" "q-148218" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{23\\sqrt{10}}{6}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6d5e6f80d4df795a749d7ac87e1a0666579b900c6d042388e113c660bec8d65"} {"question" "e-6310" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the line integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\limits_{C} \\dfrac{y^2}{x} \\,\\textrm ds,\n$$\n\n where $C$ is the section of the curve $y = \\dfrac{x^3}3$ between the points $(0,0)$ and $(3,9).$\nWrite the line integral in the form \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/example-6310.json" "file_sha256" "02165b639d6a58f7aa0bfae320121127a81216df205a2060d74e99bee0076581"} {"question" "e-6310" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y = y(t) = \\dfrac{t^3}{3}.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle t, \\dfrac{t^3}3 \\right\\rangle, \\qquad 0 \\le t \\le 3,\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3})j \\\\  & =i+{t}^{2}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{({t}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y^2}{x},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{{(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3})}^{2}}{t}=\\frac{1}{9}{t}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{9}{t}^{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{5}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/example-6310.json" "file_sha256" "02165b639d6a58f7aa0bfae320121127a81216df205a2060d74e99bee0076581"} {"question" "e-6306" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} \\dfrac{y^3}{x^2} \\, \\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is given by $y = x$ for $\\, 0 \\le x \\le 4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/example-6306.json" "file_sha256" "ecfce0cecfe1cb6be35f71d456dd0a3c52d697bc9dfe49a40203c6612911ceec"} {"question" "e-6306" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y = y(t) = t.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle t, \\: t \\right\\rangle, \\qquad 0 \\le t \\le 4\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y^3}{x^2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{{t}^{3}}{{t}^{2}}=t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{{y}^{3}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}t\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{t}^{2}{|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}({4}^{2}-{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055809/example-6306.json" "file_sha256" "ecfce0cecfe1cb6be35f71d456dd0a3c52d697bc9dfe49a40203c6612911ceec"}] "usage" {"e-6310" [] "e-6306" [] "q-76483" []} "basis" 1819 "retractions" [[17592186152666 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x, y) = 2y^{2}$ along the curve $y = e^{x}$, $0 \\le x \\le \\ln 3$."] [17592186151873 :question/problem "Consider the line integral $\\displaystyle \\int \\limits_{C} \\dfrac{y^2}{x} \\,\\textrm ds$, where $C$ is the section of the curve $y = \\dfrac{x^3}3$ between the points $(0,0)$ and $(3,9)$.\nWrite the line integral in the form $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_a^b f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t$."] [17592186151873 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x)$. Therefore, by setting $x=t$, we have\n\n$$\ny = y(t) = \\dfrac{t^3}{3}\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle t, \\dfrac{t^3}3 \\right\\rangle, \\qquad 0 \\le t \\le 3\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{t^{3}}{3})j \\\\\n&= i + t^{2}j \\\\\n‖r^{′}(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1^{2} + (t^{2})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1 + t^{4}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\dfrac{y^2}{x}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r(t)) &= ((\\frac{t^{3}}{3})^{2})/(t) = \\frac{1}{9}t^{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)f(x, y)ds &= ∫_{0}^{3}f(r(t))‖r^{′}(t)‖dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{9}t^{5} \\cdot \\sqrt{1 + t^{4}} \\cdot dt \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{9}∫_{0}^{3}t^{5}\\sqrt{1 + t^{4}}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186151872 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} \\dfrac{y^3}{x^2} \\, \\text{d}s$, where the curve $C$ is given by $y = x$ for $\\, 0 \\le x \\le 4$."] [17592186151872 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x)$. Therefore, by setting $x=t$, we have\n\n$$\ny = y(t) = t\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle t, \\: t \\right\\rangle, \\qquad 0 \\le t \\le 4\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(t)j \\\\\n&= i + j \\\\\n‖r^{′}(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1^{2} + 1^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\dfrac{y^3}{x^2}\n$$\n\n, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r(t)) &= \\frac{t^{3}}{t^{2}} = t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)\\frac{y^{3}}{x^{2}}ds &= ∫_{0}^{4}f(r(t))‖r^{′}(t)‖dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{4}t \\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\cdot dt \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2}∫_{0}^{4}tdt \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}t^{2} \\mid _{0}^{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(4^{2} - 0^{2}) \\\\\n&= 8\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186150907 17592186150908 17592186150909 17592186150910 17592186150911] "immutable_fields" [[17592186150906 :answer-field/correct] [17592186150906 :answer-field/id] [17592186150906 :answer-field/key] [17592186150906 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
